}

Posterior draws under a continuous shrinkage prior contain no exact zeros,
so "selected" is always a named decision and never a property of the
draws, and a posterior-threshold retention is not an inclusion
probability. Every comparison against an external optimiser carries one of
three equivalence levels -- \code{map_exact_fixed_covariance},
\code{posterior_predictive_comparable} or \code{no_identity_expected} -- and none of
them licenses the sentence "the same as glmnet". The mode identity a
ridge or Laplace prior has with a penalised objective holds only with the
covariance plugged in and the residual scale held fixed; the Bayesian
route integrates the covariance and samples \code{sigma}, so no arm of it
reaches \code{map_exact_fixed_covariance}.

Where the decision is \code{"projection"}, the search and the predictive
evaluation run on CLUSTERED DRAWS rather than on the whole posterior --
20 clusters for the search, 100 for the evaluation. Projecting a
multilevel submodel is a fit per draw, so projecting every draw at every
search step inside every fold does not finish on a problem of the size
this verb is for. Both counts are recorded on the result and on its
certificate and printed on the object's first line, so a reader can see
what the elpd column was computed from.

\code{penalty = NULL} keeps its earlier meaning: every candidate is protected
and the fit is the extended base model. Under \code{workflow = "bayes"} it
refits the base's own engine inside every assessment fold and scores
held-out clusters by posterior predictive draws, which is the genuinely
Bayesian no-penalty reference and the correct baseline for every shrinkage
family. The closed-form generalised-least-squares route on a plugged-in
fold covariance remains available through \code{benchmark = TRUE} and is
labelled \code{unpenalized_benchmark} wherever it appears.

Fold refits run one at a time by default.
\code{options(flexyBayes.selection_workers = n)} runs \code{n} of them at once on a
platform that forks, and divides the engine threads between the workers so
that workers times threads does not exceed the machine. Nothing the run
records changes: the fold allocation, each fold's candidate space, the
score contract, the certificate and the selected support are identical to
the serial run's, and the worker count is deliberately absent from the
certificate, which describes the estimate while the worker count describes
the machine that produced it. Engine numbers move as they move between any
two repeated runs on an engine whose posterior sampler is not
bit-reproducible. Set \code{n} against MEMORY: every concurrent fold holds its
own rows and its own engine process at the same time, so the count a
machine supports follows from its memory, and usually sits well below its
core count. Measured on the package's synthetic selection fixture with
INLA, \code{penalty = NULL} and four folds, the whole call held at its peak
1.7 GB with one worker and 3.0, 4.1 and 4.6 GB with two, three and four at
2,000 rows and 20 candidates; 2.4 GB with one worker and 3.8, 5.3 and
5.5 GB at 5,000 rows and 40 candidates; and, with three folds, 4.3 GB with
one worker and 7.9 and 11.5 GB with two and three at 10,890 rows and 82
candidates. A worker the operating system kills under that pressure
refuses by name, and the aggregation keeps every fold or none.

\code{options(flexyBayes.isolation = "process")} runs each fold in a separate R
process (see \code{\link[=flexybayes]{flexybayes()}}), one at a time, or \code{n} at once with the worker
option. A fold whose process ends before it returns refuses with
\code{isolated_process_died}, naming the fold and the signal, and this session
carries on. Each fold then pays for starting R and reading its inputs:
about 2 seconds a fold on the closed-form route, and 2.3 to 2.4 seconds a
fit for \code{flexybayes()} at 96 and 5,000 rows. At 2,000 rows and 20
candidates the call held 2.1 GB with one fold at a time and 3.8 GB with
three at once.

\code{fallback = "none"} is the default. A Bayesian request whose provider is
absent refuses with \code{selection_provider_absent}, naming the package and
the install line. \code{fallback = "glmnet"} is a deliberate choice; when it
fires, \code{workflow} becomes \code{penalized_optimizer}, the object prints the
substitution on its first line, and the certificate records
\code{fallback_fired = TRUE}. There is no configuration that produces a
penalised-likelihood answer under a Bayesian label.

Each fold's candidate space is fixed from the covariates on its training
rows before any fold is fitted. The protected columns, then the candidate
columns in the order \code{candidates} writes them, are factorised by
\code{qr()} with tolerance \code{1e-7}, every column but the intercept centred on
the training rows. A protected column aliased there refuses with
\code{selection_fold_protected_unidentified}, unless \code{protected_alias = "drop"}
(see "Aliased protected columns"), and a factor level present only in
a fold's assessment rows refuses with \code{selection_fold_level_absent}, both
before any engine runs. A candidate column aliased there is dropped for that
fold, as when a fold's training trials leave fewer degrees of freedom than
its trial-level candidates need: the fold's fits and scores use the columns
it keeps, every coefficient it reports keeps the declared columns in order
with \code{NA} for a dropped one, and the certificate's
\code{design$candidate_space} names each drop with its reason and counts, for
each candidate column, the folds in which it was estimable. The \code{glmnet}
arm's \code{selection$candidate_availability} places that count beside the
number of folds whose support holds the column at the chosen point. The
Bayesian route drops whole terms and refuses a term aliased only in part, and
the penalised reference's out-of-fold score refuses any drop, both with
\code{selection_fold_candidate_drop_not_representable}.
}
\section{Aliased protected columns}{

In an unbalanced multi-trial design some cells of a protected interaction
\code{VARIETY:NAPPLIED:SEEDRATE} are never sown, so some of its columns are
linear combinations of others, on all rows or on a fold's training rows. With \code{protected_alias = "refuse"} a fold like that refuses with
\code{selection_fold_protected_unidentified}. With \code{protected_alias = "drop"}
each fold drops them as ASReml 4.2 does: the protected columns are taken
term by term, and within a term in descending order of their factor
levels, the term's first factor varying slowest, and a column that is a
linear combination of the columns before it on the training rows is
dropped. These are the columns asreml reports as aliased, less the
first-level columns that the treatment contrasts leave out.

A held-out row whose protected row is not a linear combination of the
training rows' protected rows has no estimable prediction: its cell was
never sown on the training rows, and \code{predict()} on the asreml fit
returns \code{NA} for it. Such a row is not scored. The certificate's
\code{design$candidate_space} lists each fold's dropped protected columns in
\code{protected_dropped} and, in \code{not_estimable}, each fold's held-out rows and
how many of them were not estimable, and the printed certificate gives
the totals. A fold with no estimable held-out row refuses. The Bayesian
workflow and \code{refit = list(method = "bayes")} write the protected part as
formula text, which cannot leave out one column of a term, and both
refuse \code{"drop"}. With
\code{covariance = "base_model"} the fold's variance components come from a
refit of the base model with every protected column, under its prior.
}

\section{Bayesian refit of the frozen support}{

With \code{workflow = "penalized_optimizer"} and a penalty that selects,
\code{refit = list(method = "bayes")} takes the support the optimiser chose on
each fold at its chosen tuning point, freezes it, and refits the base model
on that fold's training rows with the base's own engine (INLA or brms),
random terms and priors, the support columns added to the protected fixed
part. Nothing is selected again. Each support column enters as a numeric
design column learned on the fold's training rows, so a fold that keeps one
column of a factor and drops another is refitted on exactly the columns it
kept.

Each assessment row is scored by its log predictive density,
\verb{log((1/D) sum_d p(y_i | theta_d))} over the refit's \code{D} posterior draws.
Under \code{prediction = "new_cluster"}, where every random term is an
independent intercept, the unseen levels' effects are integrated in closed
form: each draw's predictive is Gaussian with the fixed part's mean and the
residual variance plus the random-intercept variances. Otherwise the
engine's draws of the linear predictor are used. The per-unit sums, their
total over held-out units and its standard error \code{sqrt(U) * sd(elpd_u)}
are reported by \code{\link[=glance.fb_select]{glance.fb_select()}} and \code{\link[=augment.fb_select]{augment.fb_select()}}, with the
hash of a value contract that identifies the folds, the scoring unit, the
prediction target and the response values. Two runs on the same contract
pair unit by unit: \code{glance(x, against = y)}.

The block share follows the Bayesian R-squared of Gelman, Goodrich, Gabry
and Vehtari (2019, \doi{10.1080/00031305.2018.1549100}). For each
posterior draw of each fold's refit, with \code{m_t} the mean over the training
rows of level \code{t} of \code{group} of the block's contribution
\verb{sum_j x_ij beta_j}, the share is \code{V_t(m_t) / (V_t(m_t) + sigma^2)}: the
numerator is the sample variance of \code{m_t} over the fold's \code{T} training
levels (divisor \code{T - 1}) and \code{sigma^2} is the variance of the \code{group}
random intercept. The draws of every fold are pooled, and the posterior
median and 95\% interval reported.
}

\section{Grouped ablation}{

Under shrinkage a coefficient's size is not a column's importance.
With \code{workflow = "penalized_optimizer"}, \code{ablation = TRUE} drops each
declared block of penalised columns in turn -- the groups
\code{tidy(x, level = "group")} reports: the grouping of a grouped penalty, and
otherwise each penalty specification -- and walks the path again on the
same folds, with the same fold covariance, over the same grid of
\code{s = lambda / lambda_max}, tuned by the same rule. Each held-out unit's
loss, at the metric the path was tuned on and averaged over repeats, is
paired with its loss under the full path. \code{tidy(x, level = "group")}
reports the mean over units of the difference, the unit's loss without the
block minus with it, so positive means the block lowers the held-out loss,
and its standard error \code{sd(d_u) / sqrt(U)} over the \code{U} units. A block
whose removal leaves no penalised column leaves the protected columns,
fitted by generalised least squares on each fold. A block whose remaining
penalty the path refuses is reported with the refusal and no difference.
With \code{refit = list(method = "bayes")}, each ablated path's frozen supports
are refitted as well, and the held-out log predictive density difference,
\code{elpd} with the block minus without it, is reported beside the loss with
the valuation's standard error.

Every path is scored under one contract, which identifies the fold of
every row, the scoring unit, the target, the response and the metric, and
its hash is on the result. The read-out is not nested: each path's tuning
point is chosen on the out-of-fold scores of all folds, including the fold
whose units are then scored, and the full and ablated paths share that
reuse. On the package's synthetic two-block fixture (eight data seeds, both
rules), choosing the point on inner folds of each training fold instead
moved a signal block's difference by a median of 0.14 standard errors and
changed none of 32 verdicts. A block that carries no signal is not read as
important there, but its difference can be negative beyond one standard
error: without it the path is tuned to less shrinkage.
}

\examples{
\donttest{
if (requireNamespace("INLA", quietly = TRUE) &&
  requireNamespace("brms", quietly = TRUE)) {
  set.seed(1)
  d <- data.frame(
    site = factor(rep(1:8, each = 10)),
    x1 = rnorm(80),
    x2 = rnorm(80)
  )
  d$y <- 1 + 0.5 * d$x1 + rnorm(8)[d$site] + rnorm(80)
  base <- flexybayes(y ~ 1 + (1 | site), data = d, backend = "inla",
                     verbose = FALSE)
  # The plan resolves the axes, the prior blocks and the structure gate,
  # and estimates the cost, without fitting the reference model.
  fb_select(
    base,
    candidates = ~ x1 + x2,
    penalty = fb_penalty(term(everything()) ~ laplace(scale = 1)),
    seed = 1L,
    plan = TRUE
  )
}
}
}
